<Article
  eyebrow="Homelab · Incident"
  title="The Two Trad Towers"
  description="I broke my server editing one line of config. Then I spent five hours breaking it considerably worse trying to fix it."
  date="August 2026"
  readingTime="7 min read"
>
  <p>I run a home server. It holds the family photos, the movies, the backups,
  and a dozen small services that everyone in the house has quietly come to
  depend on without ever thinking about it. One evening in August I changed a
  single environment variable on one container and lost the entire machine for
  five hours.</p>

  <p>The config change was harmless. What cost me the evening was my reaction
  to it.</p>

  <Callout title="What actually happened" icon="◆" hue="homelab">
    <p>The container edit locked up the server. I recovered it the way everyone
    recovers a locked-up machine, by holding the power button. I did that several
    times. One of those hard cuts landed while the operating system was writing
    the file that defines its entire network configuration, and truncated it from
    twenty-two lines to six.</p>
    <p>The machine then came up with a randomly generated hardware address on
    every single boot, so my network saw a stranger where the server used to be.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What it looked like while I had no idea</h2>

  <p>The web interface would load, look completely normal for about four seconds,
  and freeze. SSH alternated between refusing the connection and timing out, with
  no pattern I could find. Plex went offline, came back, went offline again. Ping
  ran at fifty to eighty percent packet loss.</p>

  <p>That packet loss number is the part I should have paid attention to. Total
  loss would have meant something was simply down. Fifty to eighty percent
  sustained, for hours, means something is actively fighting, and I had never
  seen my network do that before.</p>

  <h2>Four hours of wrong theories</h2>

  <p>In order, and with rough time spent on each:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The container did it.</strong> Ninety minutes. I removed the
    container, removed its config, and removed two others for good measure. No
    change, which should have told me something and didn't.</li>
    <li><strong>The disks are dying.</strong> Forty minutes of reading drive
    health stats with real dread. The drives were fine. They have been fine the
    whole time.</li>
    <li><strong>The network switch is failing.</strong> An hour, including
    physically swapping a cable and a port. I want to be clear that I did this
    while the actual answer was visible on a screen in the next room.</li>
    <li><strong>The router hates me.</strong> Forty minutes of rebooting network
    equipment, which is the troubleshooting equivalent of shouting.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The answer was in my router's client list the entire time. There were two
  devices with my server's name on them. I had one server. I looked at that list
  at least three times during the first hour and my eyes went straight past it,
  because I was looking for something to be missing, and instead something had
  been added.</p>

  <h2>The actual cause</h2>

  <p>The file that defines the network bridge had been cut off partway through a
  write, which is exactly what you would expect from losing power mid-save. The
  first six lines survived. Everything that named a physical network card was
  gone.</p>

  <p>With no physical card to inherit an identity from, the operating system did
  the only sensible thing available to it and generated a random one. Every
  network device has a hardware address that is supposed to be permanent and
  unique, and most home networks quietly depend on that. Mine handed out my
  server's fixed IP address based on it.</p>

  <p>New hardware address, new device as far as the router was concerned. It
  refused to hand over the reserved address to this stranger and offered a
  different one instead.</p>

  <h3>Then it got worse without my help</h3>

  <p>The server kept insisting on the address it remembered. It would claim the
  address, hear its own claim echo back off the network, conclude that some other
  machine was impersonating it, defensively give the address up, delete its route
  to the internet, and immediately ask for the address again.</p>

  <p>Every five seconds. For hours. My server spent the evening in an argument
  with itself, and every service on it failed in that same rhythm, because
  nothing can hold a network connection open on an address that keeps evaporating.</p>

  <h2>The fix</h2>

  <p>I rewrote the config file by hand at the console with the network
  definitions restored, and set a static IP address instead of relying on the
  router's reservation. One reboot and everything came back.</p>

  <p>The static address is the part worth copying. A router reservation is a
  promise made by the router, and it is keyed to a hardware address I had assumed
  was permanent. A static address is a decision the machine makes about itself,
  and it survives the exact failure that caused all of this.</p>

  <h2>If you run something like this, check three things</h2>

  <p>None of these take more than a few minutes, and any one of them would have
  saved me an evening.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Is your critical infrastructure on a static IP, or a router
    reservation?</strong> Reservations are easier to set up and they fail in ways
    that are genuinely hard to diagnose. Anything that other machines depend on
    finding should decide its own address.</li>
    <li><strong>Do you have a backup of your boot configuration?</strong> Mine
    lives on a USB flash drive, which is a normal design for this kind of server
    and also the least durable storage in the building. I now back it up on a
    schedule. I did not before, and rewriting that file by hand was luck rather
    than preparedness.</li>
    <li><strong>Can you see your router's client list?</strong> Learn what normal
    looks like on it while nothing is wrong. An unfamiliar device is a signal you
    will only recognise if you know the baseline.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout title="The habit I actually changed" icon="⚑" hue="building">
    <p>I used to treat holding the power button as a free action. Every hard
    power cycle interrupts a write somewhere. Usually it lands on something
    unimportant and you never find out. This time it landed on the file my entire
    network depends on.</p>
    <p>I now wait a lot longer for a machine to recover on its own before I reach
    for the button, and I accept that sometimes this means waiting twenty minutes
    while feeling like an idiot.</p>
  </Callout>

  <p>Total cost: five hours, one deeply confused household, and a lasting
  suspicion of any troubleshooting step that begins with "just reboot it."</p>
</Article>
